\details{
This diagnostic relies on Marchenko--Pastur theory for the null
eigenvalue distribution of a (possibly high-dimensional) sample
covariance matrix, and is therefore only meaningful for objects of
class \code{"pca"} (i.e. objects returned by \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}). It does not apply
to sparse fits returned by \code{\link[=spca]{spca()}} (class \code{"spca"}), for which the
reference distribution does not hold; no method is defined for that
class, so calling it on such an object raises the standard
"no applicable method" error rather than producing a plot.

The Marchenko--Pastur distribution depends on the ratio of variables to
observations. The \code{gamma} argument uses its reciprocal, \code{n_obs / n_vars},
as required by \code{RMTstat::qmp}. Both counts must be available, even when
\code{gamma} is supplied. The covariance reference assumes independent
variables with a common population variance.
With \code{cor = TRUE}, the theoretical quantiles are scaled to sum to
\code{n_vars}. A warning is issued if the stored eigenvalues do not sum to
\code{n_vars} within a tolerance of \code{1e-8 * n_vars}. This check assumes that
all nonzero eigenvalues are stored; it does not establish that the
original matrix is a correlation matrix.
With \code{cor = FALSE}, the quantiles retain the scale set by \code{common_var}.
A fitted line must use at least two of the plotted points.
